Command execution and metadata publication

Status: Phases 1, 2, 2a, 3 and 3a complete after second review and independent completion audit.
The narrower command-result policy is approved. Binding repairs, DEBUG-only diagnostics and the shared
creation/publication boundary and explicit operation ownership are implemented; Phases 4–9 remain open.
Source: [approved proposal](Rendering.CommandPublication.Proposal.md).
Inherited contracts: [resource design](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Proposal.md),
[inventory](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Inventory.md), [API coverage](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.ApiCoverage.md).
Evidence: [per-method analysis](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Analysis.md).
Parent: [resource-handles checklist](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.todo), Phase 4f (delegating phase).

Goal
  Manager creation methods consume assignment-tracked initialization records and return nullable
  canonical metadata plus a success-only handle. Explicit StateCache command methods own native
  dispatch and completion. Lookup, acquisition/recovery and result publication have separate contracts.
  Preserve controlled inputs and derived facts; acquire external unknowns and specifically justified outputs.

Scope and ordering
  This checklist is the authoritative implementation sequence delegated by parent Phase 4f.
  The parent records architectural approval and gates later consumer integration on this entire checklist.
  Phase 1 defines the settled contracts; Phase 2 repairs bindings; Phase 2a confines diagnostic counters
  to DEBUG builds; Phases 3–7 (including 3a) implement creation,
  publication and acquisition; Phase 8 handles supplied-metadata preparation; Phase 9 qualifies the whole.
  Parent phase markers are not prerequisites for individual child phases or separate implementation tasks.
  Completion evidence lives here; parent consumer/integrated qualification remains later.
  No recording/queues, GpuCommand/GpuCommandList implementation, StateCache rename, new lifetime keys,
  second registry, full VAO mirror, content tracking or broader interception coverage is introduced.
  Retain GL/Core, Ext and Arb coverage and existing legacy/auxiliary-owner exclusions.
  Higher-level growth, streaming, sampling and content policy stays with existing wrappers/helpers.

Resolved direction
  Controlled inputs/defaults/derivations are authoritative under existing failure handling. There is
  no universal post-command verification or duplicate requested/actual snapshot system. The proposal
  specifies external acquisition, narrow justified outputs, initialization defaults and wrapper handoff.
  Preserve existing suppression, lifecycle checks and reported-failure cleanup. Silent native errors
  are not newly detected by nullable returns; use existing diagnostics and explicit recovery.

Phase 1: Settle creation and command-result contracts
  Sources: proposal, Initialization records, Separate three operations, Command execution, Transactions;
  analysis, Binding paths, Native observation pairs and acquisition/reader findings; inherited contracts.
  Outcome: concrete contracts permit implementation without hidden acceptance or ownership assumptions.

  ☑ For every API coverage family and analyzed observer, specify established argument-derived facts,
    specifically justified output reads and unknown-state recovery. Distinguish managed
    commands from arbitrary intercepted calls, including rejected and partially successful multi-calls.
    Preserve native errors; no blanket GetError consumption or blanket Is-object queries.
  ☑ Agree this result policy with the user before implementing it. Record resolved decisions in the
    proposal; architecture approval alone does not settle this policy.
  ☑ Define each initialization record's legal inputs, automatic private assignment mask, required values,
    conditional GL defaults, explicit creation-policy defaults and derivable outputs. Reject missing
    required allocation inputs instead of attempting to query them. Explicit zero/false differs from omission.
  ☑ Specify nullable metadata plus out GpuResourceHandle success/failure semantics and the wrapper handoff:
    manager-owned association and HandleMetadataChanged delivery, no wrapper snapshot assignment,
    no leaked handle between creation and association, and cleanup of provisional state on handoff failure.
    Preserve generated-name versus realized-object and context-free declaration contracts.
  ☑ Specify internal operation/group/deferred state: original entry/context capture, exactly-once completion,
    affected availability, nested/alias grouping, interruption and recovery. Define context-local execution
    through the existing static manager path without a second registry or wrapper tracking state machine.
  ☑ Reconcile proposal/inventory/API mechanics with this approved refinement. Retain the parent consumer
    gate without duplicating implementation tasks; record alternatives and reasons
    already described in the proposal rather than silently retaining the old full-refresh contract.
  ☑ Completion: remaining decisions are explicit, the result policy is agreed, source contracts agree,
    and tasks below have no unresolved foundational prerequisite. No runtime change is required here.

Phase 2: Centralize selector execution and repair binding knowledge
  Depends on Phase 1. Sources: proposal Command execution; analysis Binding paths and Scopes;
  API selector/routing semantics.
  Outcome: acquisition can rely on correct binding scopes without unnecessary result rediscovery.

  ☑ Refactor UseProgramBinding, BindVertexArray and BindProgramPipeline into self-contained execution
    paths under the agreed policy. Preserve managed equal suppression and full-program stage semantics.
  ☑ Repair BindBuffer(ElementArrayBuffer), VertexArrayElementBuffer and external buffer/vertex/program
    selection observation. Maintain per-VAO EBO facts independently of VAO revision notifications.
  ☑ Repair GpuVertexAttribBinding constructors, BindVertexBuffer, SetDivisor and RequireCurrentFacts:
    keep established helper results, preserve stale-copy and original-owner checks, recover only unknown
    fields. Do not introduce a full VAO layout mirror or weaken non-DSA stale-helper rejection.
  ☑ Repair BindFramebufferScope capture to use known independent read/draw selections. Review
    BindScopeTracker, BindBufferScope, BindTextureScope and CaptureBoundaryBindings restoration against
    the same policy. Correct TryGetBufferBindingQuery's AtomicCounterBuffer binding token with a named enum.
  ☑ Migrate selector/routing adapters to shared execution bookkeeping while preserving exact overloads,
    actual external dispatch, errors, reentrancy and unknown-state recovery. Keep read/draw routing distinct.
  ☑ Have subagents verify changed/equal/unknown/rejected bindings, external changes, DSA/fallback,
    scopes, deletion/name reuse and helper revisions. Compare query/bind counts with analysis receipts.
    Run affected builds/tests and canonical package checks; second-review and independently audit this binding increment.
  ☑ Completion: binding restoration and metadata notifications remain correct; verified binding/query
    baseline is available for subsequent acquisition work. No performance inference from counts alone.

  Verification: Release passed 263/263; Debug passed 262/262 plus the added reentrancy test (1/1),
  with no skipped cases. Second review and independent completion audit passed.
  Receipts: artifacts/command-publication-phase2-results contains complete-suite and focused TRX files.
  Canonical ./build.ps1 --configuration=Release --spirv=true passed; tested/published/packaged runtime
  hashes match and all 761 packaged SPIR-V files match tested/generated outputs (zero mismatches).
  See artifacts/command-publication-phase2-package-complete.log and
  artifacts/command-publication-phase2-package-identity.json. Native probes retain controlled
  selector changes with zero post-bind getters (previously one each), warm split-framebuffer scopes
  with zero getters (previously two), cold read-only scopes with one getter, and authored helper
  updates with zero indexed getters (previously four per returned helper). Stale DSA fields recover
  individually; registered and context-local helpers, fallback rejection, external errors, routing,
  reentrant selection, owner restoration, deletion and original-owner checks retain coverage.
  Counts establish command/query behavior, not CPU/GPU timing or live-game acceptance.
  Existing qualification limitation: EngineStateSwitchingTests has an order-dependent discovery
  failure for generic Update<T>. The same full fixture fails on isolated unchanged HEAD (7 pass,
  1 fail), while its discovery test alone passes. See artifacts/command-publication-phase2-head-switching-suite.log;
  this does not change Phase 2's selector coverage and remains an integrated-qualification concern.

Phase 2a: Confine rendering diagnostic counters to DEBUG builds
  Depends on Phase 2. Source: user-approved diagnostic-counter cleanup and DEBUG-only build policy.
  Outcome: ordinary Release builds contain no diagnostic counter storage or updates; a no-op profiler facade and empty value scope keep
  render call sites configuration-independent. Counters required
  for rendering correctness remain available in every configuration. No instrumented Release variant,
  alternative instrumentation symbol, build switch or runtime counter-enablement mechanism is introduced.

  ☑ Review counters across production Rendering and trace their readers before classifying them.
    Preserve metadata/storage/executable/content revisions, VertexConfigurationEpoch, lifetime references,
    scope depth, queue occupancy/sequences, allocation budgets and fence serial/completion bookkeeping.
    These determine correctness and must not be guarded by DEBUG. Remove confirmed unread diagnostic
    counters, including GpuComputePipeline.spirvUsedCount, instead of retaining unused Debug instrumentation.
  ☑ Guard StateCache diagnostic declarations and all increments/accumulation with #if DEBUG:
    ResourceSlotBindCount, TextureBindCount, SamplerBindCount, TextureCacheChecks, SamplerCacheChecks,
    ImageCacheChecks, IndexedBufferCacheChecks, FixedFunctionCalls, BoundaryQueries and BoundaryErrorChecks.
    Keep actual bindings, cache comparisons, native queries, error checks and restoration unconditional.
  ☑ Apply the same separation to GpuSupport.CaptureCount, ShaderInputValidation.CompatibilityChecks,
    GpuPreparedBindings/GraphicsExecutableInterface.ReflectionQueries, GpuUniformRingBuffer allocation/byte
    totals, PersistentUniformStorage allocation/byte/binding/fence-poll totals and ShaderLinkBatch.ConsumedCount.
    Preserve capability capture, validation/reflection, uniform uploads, fence polling and shader-link
    progression themselves. Retain ResidentBytes and other budget-enforcement state in Release.
  ☑ Review TextureStreamingManager telemetry and GlGpuProfiler statistics and their consumers. Guard
    diagnostic-only accumulation and reporting paths with DEBUG while retaining
    upload budgets, pending-work counts, ordering, scheduling and resource retirement in Release.
    Keep GlGpuProfiler and its scope API available in both configurations: conditional void operations
    and an empty Release scope contain the build policy inside the profiler. Scope callers need no
    preprocessor guards. Measurement state, GPU queries and diagnostic UI remain DEBUG-only.
    Reconcile any mixed diagnostic/functional snapshot consumers without substituting fabricated zero
    statistics or disabling functional work along with telemetry.
  ☑ Update affected tests and diagnostic consumers to compile in both configurations. Counter assertions
    belong to Debug tests; keep functional rendering, state-restoration, error and lifetime assertions
    active in Release. Do not define DEBUG for Release or add an instrumented Release test configuration.
    Apply this DEBUG-only policy to diagnostic counters introduced by subsequent checklist work.
  ☑ Have subagents build and run affected Debug and ordinary Release suites and canonical Release package
    checks. Verify Debug instrumentation still works, and review the compiled Release artifact to confirm
    diagnostic members and update operations are absent while functional counters remain. Reconcile
    affected documentation, perform a second review and independently audit the configuration boundary.
  ☑ Completion: diagnostic counters are DEBUG-only or removed when unused; Release rendering retains
    all required behavior and bookkeeping, tests cover both configurations, and no instrumentation-enabled
    Release build path exists.

