Phase 3a: Replace ambient groups with operation-owned publication
  Depends on Phase 3. Sources: proposal Operation-owned publication and Shared commit and interruption
  contract. The completed Phase 3 receipts describe the earlier implementation; they do not qualify this
  replacement. This phase supersedes its automatic nested/alias grouping contract. Complete before Phase 4.

  ☑ Replace GpuResourceManager.MetadataGroups.cs, MetadataGroup<T>/Lease, entry.ActiveUpdate and the
    thread-local localMetadataGroups lookup with narrowly scoped operation-owned state. Preserve original
    entry/context identity, affected availability, combined effects and exactly-once commit/failure handling.
    Share the contract for registered and context-local resources through existing manager services.
  ☑ Make PublishMetadata/PublishObservedMetadata commit complete supplied candidates unconditionally with
    respect to grouping; make RefreshMetadata/ObserveWrapperMetadata explicit recovery paths. Remove
    hidden stage/defer branches in publication, acquisition and texture generation. A busy guard may reject
    access/reentry but must not act as an ambient collector or second resource registry.
  ☑ Move ownership of Allocate2DStorage, layered allocation, SetMipRange and framebuffer attachment-update
    sequences from wrapper-held BeginTextureStorageUpdate/BeginTextureMetadataUpdate/
    BeginFramebufferAttachmentUpdate leases into explicit StateCache operations. Preserve wrapper policy,
    owning APIs, DSA/fallback behavior and binding restoration. Internal steps receive operation state
    explicitly and contribute facts/effects without independent publication; wrappers do not author metadata.
  ☑ Keep newly created resources unpublished until the existing CreateResource handoff commits a complete
    result. Do not introduce alias groups for creation or pull family-specific initialization migrations
    forward from Phases 4–6. Preserve nullable failures, cleanup and borrowed-dependency ownership.
  ☑ Reject unrelated same-resource reentrant mutations, including alias calls, before native dispatch.
    Explicitly participating nested steps share failure state; a caught participating failure still prevents
    commit. Deletion/context retirement cancels original completion; reused IDs cannot redirect it.
    Preserve unrelated bindings and independent resource outcomes; do not roll back native commands.
  ☑ Keep texture generation/query policy in its operation and framebuffer reconciliation/dependency
    notification in manager services. Remove resource-specific branches and reader selection from shared
    commit machinery. For unmigrated families, invoke necessary existing readers explicitly in the owning
    operation, at most once per complete result; no recovery-on-disposal or additional queries. Input-derived
    family migrations remain Phases 4–7. Keep deferred linking under its separate existing owner.
  ☑ Preserve manager-owned local snapshot discard and image invalidation on failure, combined revisions
    and snapshot-before-notification ordering. Dispose only cleans bookkeeping; explicit success commits.
    Publication failure must not retry native mutation or replace an original dispatch exception.
  ☑ Replace tests requiring automatic alias-group joining with behavioral tests for explicit composition,
    final alias delivery, pre-dispatch reentry rejection, caught nested failure, deletion/name reuse,
    context retirement, local failure, exactly-once publication and recovery boundaries. Have subagents
    run focused Debug/Release tests and relevant binding/resource regressions; verify dispatch/query counts
    and canonical package identity, documenting existing Release observer limitations accurately.
  ☑ Reconcile current-state inventory and affected architecture documentation after implementation; audit
    source/callers for surviving ambient group dependencies. Obtain an independent completion audit.
  ☑ Completion: explicit operations own coherent results; publication and recovery have stable meanings;
    no ambient joining, thread-local group registry, generic reader fallback or wrapper-authored metadata
    remains. Family migrations can build on the revised boundary; later tasks remain unchecked.

